            <ArticleTitle>IgG myeloma presenting as a large mediastinal mass and pleural effusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>65-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/LBR.0b013e31823fcc5b</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Plasmacytoma, a rare form of plasma cell dyscrasia, refers to a neoplastic proliferation of plasma cells. Although the upper aerodigestive tract is the most common site for extramedullary plasmacytoma, mediastinal and pleural involvement is extremely rare. We report the case of a patient who presented with a large mediastinal mass and pleural effusion. Diagnosis was challenging and subsequently made as mediastinal extramedullary plasmacytoma with simultaneous myelomatous pleural effusion. Further investigations revealed multiple myeloma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
